\section{Introduction}

The rapid expansion of the Internet of Things (IoT) has led to the deployment of vast numbers of sensors across diverse sectors, including healthcare, smart grids, manufacturing, transportation, and cybersecurity. These devices generate and process substantial data volumes, creating ideal conditions for machine learning. However, these data are typically distributed across multiple locations, forming distributed machine learning (DML) scenarios. For instance, in cybersecurity, a network intrusion detection system (NIDS) distributed across several sites requires access to localized data that centralization cannot provide. Although model extraction is theoretically possible, it is often impractical due to privacy concerns, intellectual property protection, and the high costs and complexities associated with data transfer. Consequently, training a NIDS centrally is difficult, making federated learning an attractive alternative for such scenarios.

Federated learning (FL) is a distributed approach where data remains localized on client devices while global models are aggregated by a server. This method has seen significant interest recently, particularly in federated deep learning. Recent literature has explored FL applications across various fields, including federated transfer learning and federated reinforcement learning. To our knowledge, our work is the first to examine the limitations of FL specifically for cybersecurity classification. We focus on two key questions: Does FL achieve near-centralized learning performance in cybersecurity? And do FL models generalize well to unseen data?

To address these questions, we conducted experiments across diverse cybersecurity datasets. We developed a evaluation framework incorporating standard metrics and the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence to measure data distribution differences. This framework allowed us to characterize dataset dissimilarities and report FL evaluation results. Our findings reveal a substantial generalization gap between FL and centralized models. We attribute this to dataset heterogeneity, as FL models overfit to the average class distribution of the combined data. This phenomenon, where models overfit to data aggregates, is a known challenge in FL that we highlight as a critical issue for the field.

